    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "fair enough. but even looking at the \"success\" side you mentioned earlier... i don't see many real strategists actually arguing it worked. people say it was about \"protecting civilians,\" but that feels more like a moral justification than an actual geopolitical win. do you think the \"proponents\" were actually looking at strategy, or were they just using humanitarian reasons to mask a lack of a real plan?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is likely that the \"proponents\" were framing the intervention primarily through a liberal, humanitarian lens, which often blurs the line between moral imperative and hard political strategy.\n\nFor some, the \"win\" was defined very narrowly, such as the successful application of force to achieve a specific, front-end political objective like toppling the regime, without necessarily planning for the complex, long-term state-building required for a true \"win.\"\n\nThis often highlights a common debate in political science regarding the difference between **tactic** (the successful overthrow of the target) and **strategy** (the predictable, stable outcome that follows the action).",
      "source": "target",
